<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mohammed V of Morocco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1909">1909</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mohammed V · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaouite-dynasty">Alaouite dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mohammed V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> was sultan from 1927 and the first king of independent Morocco. He supported the nationalist movement against the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, and his deposition and exile by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1953 provoked widespread unrest. Restored in 1955, he negotiated independence in 1956, took the title of king in 1957 and reigned until his death in 1961, when his son Hassan II succeeded.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mohammed-v-of-morocco.jpg" alt="Mohammed V of Morocco" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fotograaf Onbekend / Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohammed_V_(1953).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad bin Yusef was born in Fes on 10 August 1909, the third and youngest son of Sultan Yusef and Lalla Yacout. In March 1912 the Treaty of Fes made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> a French protectorate, and the French enthroned his father in September of that year. He studied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> in the palace at Fes and, after the court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>, with private teachers there, until he became a hafiz. He also studied French. He was enthroned on 18 November 1927, at the age of 17, after his father's death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In May 1930 the young sultan signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-dahir">Berber Dahir</a>, which changed the legal system in the areas where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-languages">Berber languages</a> were mainly spoken. Moroccan nationalists criticised it sharply, and it gave rise to the Moroccan nationalist movement. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he took part in the Anfa Conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casablanca">Casablanca</a> and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> on 22 January 1943. Most scholars stress his benevolence towards Moroccan Jews under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a>, since he reportedly refused to approve measures to deport them, though he did sign two decrees barring Jews from certain schools and positions. The historian Michel Abitol holds that his role has been exaggerated.</p><p>After the war he became a central figure of the independence cause. In speeches at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> on 9 and 10 April 1947 he appealed for independence and stressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>'s ties with the Arab world. Relations with the French worsened, and on 20 August 1953 the French authorities deposed him and sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, later moving him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. They installed Muhammad Ben Aarafa, a cousin, in his place. The deposition sparked active opposition, and he returned on 16 November 1955. In 1956 he negotiated Moroccan independence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and in 1957 he took the title of king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Muhammad V died on 26 February 1961, aged 51, of cardiac arrest after an operation on his nasal septum. His son Moulay Hassan, who succeeded him as Hassan II, announced his death on national radio, and a seven-day period of mourning followed. A mausoleum for his remains was built in 1971. Many streets, schools, an airport and a university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> bear his name, and historians often describe him as a moderate and conciliatory ruler.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad V of Morocco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>France's exile of Sultan <b>Mohammed V</b> to Madagascar in 1953 set off active resistance to both protectorates, with the worst violence at Oujda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty survived, and after independence in 1956 <b>Mohammed V</b> took the title of king, forming the Kingdom of Morocco.</blockquote><a href="/state/alaouite-sultanate">Alaouite Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>France deposed and exiled <b>Sultan Mohammed</b> V in 1953, provoking violent unrest.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-in-morocco">French protectorate in Morocco</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fez, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.04,-5&amp;year=1942" aria-label="Fez on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4423 930 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mohammed V of Morocco"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4873 1200h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4873 1200h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.04,-5&amp;year=1942">What was here in 1942? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
